# FIPS DLL staging in `build.rs` (workspace-wide rule)

Every binary crate that depends on `aws-lc-rs = { features = ["fips"] }`
(directly or transitively) imports `aws_lc_fips_<version>_crypto.dll`
(currently `aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll`) at runtime. Cargo builds
that DLL via the `aws-lc-fips-sys` build script and emits a
`cargo:rustc-link-search` so the import library resolves at link time —
but Cargo does **NOT** copy the DLL next to the produced binary.

MSI scripts under `pim-installers/` copy it into
`%ProgramFiles%\AIC\OfflinePim{Server,Agent}\`, so installed copies work.
But `cargo run`, `cargo test`, Playwright-driven E2E, and launching
`target\<profile>\<bin>.exe` directly all fail on Windows with
`The code execution cannot proceed because aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll
was not found.`

## Required pattern

Every binary crate (and every cdylib shipping as an executable
component) under `c:\analog-pim\*` that links `aws-lc-rs/fips` MUST have
a `build.rs` containing the **`stage_aws_lc_fips_dll`** helper. The
canonical copy lives in `pim-offline-server-configurator-win32/build.rs`
and is duplicated byte-for-byte across consumers (the workspace is
multi-repo, so a shared `[build-dependencies]` crate would need
cross-repo path patches). Keep the copies in sync.

The helper:

1. Finds the most recently modified
   `target/<profile>/build/aws-lc-fips-sys-*/out/build/artifacts/aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll`,
   scanning every hash-suffixed sibling (an incremental build can leave
   the "live" hash dir empty while the DLL survives in an older one).
2. Copies it to `target/<profile>/<dll>` for direct execution and
   `cargo run`.
3. Copies it to `target/<profile>/deps/<dll>` for `cargo test` binaries.
4. Is idempotent (size + mtime check), gated on
   `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`, no-op elsewhere.
5. Emits `cargo:warning=…` on every recoverable failure — never panics.

Staging applies to **whichever profile is being built**. While the
workspace is in development mode the DLL must be staged next to the
`target\debug` binaries too — see
[`development-mode-debug-builds.mdc`](development-mode-debug-builds.mdc).

Any new binary crate that pulls in `aws-lc-rs/fips` adds the same helper.

## How to verify

```powershell
# Clear the staged DLL and force build.rs to re-run
Remove-Item target\debug\aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item target\debug\deps\aws_lc_fips_*_crypto.dll -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
(Get-Item build.rs).LastWriteTime = Get-Date

cargo build
Test-Path target\debug\aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll
Test-Path target\debug\deps\aws_lc_fips_0_13_14_crypto.dll
```

Both `Test-Path` calls return `True`; crypto tests pass.

## Related rules

- [`never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc`](never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc) — never delete
  the upstream artifacts this helper copies from.
- [`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) §4 — NIST
  SC-13; the FIPS DLL is the validated cryptographic module boundary.
- [`no-panic.mdc`](no-panic.mdc) — the helper warns and returns.
- Per-product MSI scripts under `pim-installers/scripts/` remain the
  source of truth for the **installed** copy; this rule covers the
  developer / test workflow.
